export anchor PluginApi:
    description:
        A plugin's code imports @piton/plugin, which is bundled with the
        compiler, and exports what definePlugin returns: a handler for each
        command its declaration lists. A handler gets a context and the
        command's arguments, and returns the exit code; returning nothing is
        0.

        ```typescript
        \\\
        import { definePlugin, type Context, type Json } from "@piton/plugin";

        async function hello(ctx: Context, args: Record<string, Json>) {
          const compiled = ctx.compile({ workspace: true });
          for (const anchor of compiled.anchors({ exported: true, withinSourceRoot: true })) {
            ctx.stdout(`${anchor.name}\n`);
          }
          ctx.report.done("listed the anchors");
          return 0;
        }

        export default definePlugin({ commands: { hello } });
        \\\
        ```

        The module is written in TypeScript with its types, so it is also the
        reference an author reads. It is in the compiler's source at
        crates/piton-plugin/api/index.ts.

    hooks:
        Beside its command handlers, what definePlugin returns can hold a hook
        for each part a plugin takes in the compiler's own work. Each is
        declared in the plugin's declaration, so the host knows without running
        the plugin which plugins to ask.

        build: The plan of what `piton build` writes, for a plugin that declares build: true. Described in plugin build.
        inspect: What is out of date in the project, for a plugin that declares inspect: true. Described in plugin inspection.
        reach: The anchors the plugin reaches itself, as a list of anchors, each a file and a name, for a plugin that declares reach: true. `piton reach` and the language server count them as roots beside the entry. Described in plugin reach.
        exports: What plugins that require this one can use. Described in plugin dependencies.

    editors:
        @piton/plugin exists only inside Piton's loader, so an editor's
        TypeScript can't find it on its own and reports the import as a
        missing module. A plugin gives it a tsconfig.json whose paths map
        @piton/plugin to the API's file; then the editor and tsc check the
        plugin against the API. The test plugin's tsconfig.json, in
        plugins/test, does this with strict checking. The configuration is
        for editors only: Piton never reads it, and nothing is emitted.

    context:
        What a handler can do. Each method that reaches the machine is
        checked against the plugin's capabilities, and throws when the plugin
        didn't declare what it needs.

        plugin: The plugin's name.
        terminal: Whether stdin, stdout and stderr are terminals, the terminal's width in columns, and whether colour is shown, which NO_COLOR turns off.
        project: The project the command runs in, from the piton.config.pi in the working directory: its root, source root, entry, output directory and configuration path, and Belay's code root, shape root and adapters when it configures Belay. Paths are absolute.
        settings: The project's settings for the plugin, or null when it lists none. With them come the properties of every anchor they name, like the adapters a belay-config lists, and where each of those anchors is declared.
        compile: Compiles the project. With workspace, every source under the root, not only what the entry reaches, as the language server does.
        spawn: Starts a program the plugin may run, with arguments, and optionally a working directory inside the project and environment variables. The program runs in the project root with nothing on its stdin.
        exec: Runs a program the plugin may run with the terminal, so its input, output and prompts are the user's, and returns its exit code when it ends.
        argumentSize: spawn and exec refuse an argument or environment value too large for the operating system, which on Linux is 128 KiB or more, before the program starts. The error names which one and its size. A plugin that has more to pass writes it to a temporary file.
        applyEdits: Writes proposed edits, each a file in the project and its whole new contents, for a command the user ran to make them. Each path has to be among the edits the plugin's capabilities list. Returns the paths changed.
        choose: Asks the user to choose one item from a menu on the terminal, and gives the item chosen, or nothing when they cancel or stdin or stdout isn't a terminal. Needs the interactive capability.
        resource: The text of a file in the plugin's own directory, like a prompt it ships with. No capability is needed: the files are the plugin's.
        resources: Every file under a directory of the plugin's own, recursively, as paths relative to it with forward slashes, sorted, like the templates the init plugin ships with. No capability is needed.
        ask: Asks the user to type an answer on the terminal, and gives the answer, or nothing when they cancel or stdin or stdout isn't a terminal. Needs the interactive capability.
        chooseMany: Asks the user to check any items of a list on the terminal, some checked to start with and at least a number of them, and gives the indexes checked, or nothing when they cancel or stdin or stdout isn't a terminal. Needs the interactive capability.
        format: Piton source in the canonical format, as `piton format` writes it.
        scaffold: Writes a new project's files into a directory named relative to where piton was run, never over a file, and prints each path written; when any file exists, writes none and gives the ones that exist. Needs the scaffold capability.
        tether: Tethers the dependencies a new project's configuration declares, and says none, installed or failed; asked only to check, it says pending when there are some. Needs the scaffold capability.
        onPath: Whether a program the plugin may run is on PATH.
        readFile: The text of a file inside the project.
        stat: Whether a path inside the project is a file, a directory or something else, its size in bytes and whether it is executable, following symbolic links; null when nothing is there. Like readFile, it needs files: read.
        temporaryFile: Writes text to a new temporary file and returns its path. It is removed when the command ends.
        sleep: A promise that resolves after a number of milliseconds.
        paint: Text in a style, error, warning, success, help, note, name, heading or dim, which is colour on a terminal and plain text otherwise.
        stdout and stderr: Write text, removing colour wherever the compiler's own output would.
        stderrRaw: Writes to stderr exactly as given, terminal control codes included, for a status block a command redraws.
        report: Problems, held back and printed after everything else with the count last, as every command does: error, warning, help and note; and done and stopped, the outcome line.

    anchors:
        A compilation's anchors are read with a query. Every part of it is
        optional, and the anchors that match come ordered by the file they are
        declared in, then by name.

        extends: Only anchors with this one in their inheritance chain, named by its module and its name, like the module @piton/test and the name Test. The anchor itself isn't included. A module nothing loaded matches nothing.
        exported: Only anchors declared with export.
        concrete: Only anchors that aren't abstract.
        withinSourceRoot: Only anchors declared in the project's own source, not in tethers/ or a bundled package.

        This is how the test plugin finds every test: every exported,
        concrete anchor in the source root that extends Test, whatever keyword
        or chain it got there by.

        Each anchor comes with its name, the file it is declared in, whether
        it is exported and abstract, the keyword it was declared with, its
        resolved properties, and its location: where it names a property
        itself, or else where its name is, to point a diagnostic at.

        exports: The ids of the anchors the entry exports, in the order it exports them, the ones it forwards from other modules included.
        values: An anchor's resolved properties in their order, with nothing flattened, as described under typed values.
        slot: Where an anchor's property gets its value: the anchor whose declaration writes it, the anchor itself or one it inherits from, whether a value was given or only a type, and where that declaration names the property. Null when the anchor has no such property. A diagnostic about an inherited value points there.

    values:
        Properties arrive as JSON. A string is its plain text, with references
        written as the names they point at. A number, boolean, null, list and
        dictionary are themselves, and an implicit list is a list. An anchor
        used as a value is not inlined, which could repeat a large part of
        the project, but named, as an object with $anchor and its name, and a
        reference is an object with $reference and the name it points at.

    typedValues:
        JSON merges things some plugins have to tell apart: text that holds
        references and plain text, a dictionary and a block that mixes keys
        with prose or list items, and keys that look like numbers, which
        JavaScript puts first. So a compilation also gives an anchor's
        properties as typed values, each an object with a kind. Null,
        boolean and number carry their value. Text carries its plain text and
        the references it holds, each the id of the anchor and the property
        path. A list carries its items, and a dictionary its entries as pairs
        of key and value, in order. A mixed block carries its items, where a
        key written in the block is an entry with its key and value. An
        anchor used as a value carries its id and name, and a reference its
        id, path and the name it is written as. With these a plugin can
        follow references itself, through anchors embedded by value.

    slicing:
        A compilation can print the slice of a target, as `piton slice` does,
        citing where a layout puts each declaration instead of the source:
        the same layout a link space takes. That is how a plugin that compiles
        documents offers a slice of what it wrote, like `piton belay slice`.
        It returns the exit code, and reports what `piton slice` reports.

    diagnostics:
        A problem reported with a location is shown with the source it points
        at, like the compiler's own diagnostics, under a code the plugin
        names, or the plugin's name. A compilation's own diagnostics are
        printed with reportDiagnostics, as `piton check` prints them, and it
        says whether any were errors.

    processes:
        A process's output is read a line at a time as it is written, from
        stdout and stderr both, each line saying which it came from. Reading
        ends when the process has exited and every line has been read, and
        its exit code is then known, or null when a signal stopped it. Several
        processes run at once by awaiting them together.
